<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262). This repo is a `coordination-kit` receiver: it holds
    the shared coordination skills and the `fsgg-coord` client on disk. It used to take them by
    byte-copy sync PR (`coordination-propagate`); it now REFERENCES the versioned
    `FS.GG.Kit` package and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin. A hub change reaches this repo
    as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin (in Directory.Packages.props), and `kit-materialize.yml`
    re-runs the materialize on that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT in the solution, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize explicitly:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord, scripts/skill-view, scripts/lib/args.sh,
    scripts/lib/roots.sh) are COMMITTED: the materialize is the write arm that replaces the byte-copy,
    not a live per-build input, so it does NOT run on every build (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false, per
    the .github#1280 review). The committed bytes are content-addressed and byte-identical to canonical,
    so `coordination-coherence` stays green.

    `.config/dotnet-tools.json` IS NOT IN THAT LIST, AND SINCE FS.GG.Kit 0.18.0 IT IS THIS REPO'S OWN
    FILE (FS-GG/.github#1615, ADR-0068, 2026-07-28). That line named it until this change, and the claim
    had gone false: #1615 deleted `dist/dotnet/.config/dotnet-tools.json` from the hub's `kit:` block, so
    the materialize no longer writes the manifest, no longer owns its bytes, and no longer grades them —
    this repo's materialized set went 28 files to 27. The file is still tracked here, unchanged; what
    changed is who authors it. RENOVATE BUMPS `fs.gg.coord.cli` IN IT DIRECTLY now, with no hub
    involvement: renovate's nuget manager ships `/(^|/)dotnet-tools\.json$/` as one of its four
    `managerFilePatterns`, so .github/renovate.json's `extends` of the org preset is already enough and
    nothing here has to opt in. That is the whole point of #1615 — the kit is content-addressed, so
    bumping the engine by one integer used to edit kit content, stale `registry/repos.lock`, red
    `kit-published-coherence` on the hub's `main`, and oblige a republish plus a seven-receiver fan-out
    to move one version string in one JSON file.

    EDITING THIS FILE BY HAND IS NOW POSSIBLE, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY A CENTRAL ASSERTION REPLACED THE
    ARRANGEMENT. While the manifest was a kit row, "a repo that receives the `fsgg-coord` shim can
    actually run the engine that shim execs" (#1077) held BY CONSTRUCTION — the two rows rode one
    fabric and `repos.sh validate` refused to let them separate. Ownership moving here dissolves that
    guarantee, so ADR-0068 replaced it with `scripts/repos-audit.sh`'s ENGINE-MANIFEST SWEEP, which
    runs daily (repos-audit.yml, 08:17 UTC) over EVERY `coordination-kit` receiver's actual
    `.config/dotnet-tools.json` and asserts it declares `fs.gg.coord.cli` with a usable version.
    Deleting this file, or dropping the `fs.gg.coord.cli` entry out of it, is therefore CAUGHT
    CENTRALLY AND BY NAME within a day — not discovered by somebody here whose `fsgg-coord` invocation
    dies at tool resolution. The replacement is strictly stronger than the rule it retired: the old one
    was f(hub roster) and could never read this tree, so a receiver that hand-deleted its own manifest
    stayed green forever. Note what the sweep deliberately does NOT assert — that the pinned engine is
    the NEWEST published one. #1077's invariant was "can run the engine at all", and widening it would
    red the fleet on the day the engine ships a version.

    AND THIS REPO IS NOT AN INCIDENTAL SUBJECT OF THAT SWEEP — IT IS ONE OF THE TWO IT WAS WRITTEN FOR.
    Before #1077 the manifest rode the `build-config` fabric (4 receivers) while the `fsgg-coord` shim
    rode the coordination kit (6), and FS.GG.Audio and FS.GG.Templates were the two in the gap: they
    held the shim and had NO ENGINE TO EXEC. That is not an accident of rollout and it is not fixed by
    onboarding us — as the block below says, this repo deliberately does NOT `receive: build-config`,
    because it hand-authors its own Directory.Build.props / Directory.Packages.props, and ADR-0068
    explicitly rejected "move the manifest to build-config and onboard Templates and Audio" for that
    reason. So the structural reason we were #1077's victim is still true today, and the sweep is the
    only thing standing where the co-fabric rule used to. A future reader wondering why a daily hub job
    reads this repo's tool manifest is looking at the answer: this repo is the case.

    This repo does NOT `receive: build-config` (it hand-authors its own Directory.Build.props /
    Directory.Packages.props), so FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig stays off (its default) — the kit
    materialize never touches those files.

    ONE RUNTIME ROOT IS NO LONGER MATERIALIZED INTO (FS-GG/.github#1720, ADR-0067 §9 phase 4, stage 3,
    2026-07-28). The skills are materialized and committed at `.claude/skills` ONLY. `.agents/skills` is
    a VIEW root: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both runtimes, but git-ignored and
    RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout instead of being a second committed copy of the same
    bytes. See the FsggKitViewSkillRoots block below and FsggAudioGenerateSkillView at the bottom.
    `coordination-coherence` narrows with it — 51 graded files to 28, measured — and stays green,
    because it derives its roots from FsggKitSkillRoots. (Both numbers are AT KIT 0.17.0, which is what
    was measured. The count is 27 at 0.18.0: the retired `.config/dotnet-tools.json` row above is the
    28th file, and it is now this repo's own rather than a graded one.)

    THIS REPO'S OWN 16 SKILLS WERE IN BOTH ROOTS AND STAY VISIBLE IN BOTH. Unlike FS.GG.Templates
    (whose audited roots held only the kit's four), Audio's `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills` each
    held the same 20 directories: the kit's 4 plus 16 `fs-gg-sdd-*` skills this repo owns. `diff -r`
    between the two roots was SILENT before this change, so nothing lived only in the retired copy and
    nothing had to be relocated first — the view reproduces all 20. That check is not optional: on
    FS.GG.SDD the same diff found a tracked, producer-authoritative file with no home under a view
    (FS.GG.SDD#771), which is why the retirement order now requires it before any receiver is retired.

    `.codex/skills` IS NOT TOUCHED BY THIS CHANGE. It is the RETIRED root (ADR-0067 §5,
    FS-GG/.github#1636); FS.GG.Kit's retired-root sweep already withdrew the kit-owned files from it,
    and the 16 `fs-gg-sdd-*` skills remaining there are this repo's OWN. ADR-0065 §Retiring a root
    forbids anyone hand-deleting them, and this change does not.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. Matches the repo's band (global.json). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own (the gate locks the solution, not this). -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!-- ADR-0067 §9 phase 4, stage 3 (FS-GG/.github#1720). `.agents/skills` is a VIEW root now, not a
         materialized one: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both runtimes, but its content
         is RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout by `scripts/skill-view generate` instead of being
         a second committed copy. THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED — ADR-0065 §A root's three
         dispositions makes it the UNION of these two properties, and that union is still ADR-0011's
         two roots. Nothing is retired here; one copy stopped existing.

         WHY `.claude/skills` IS THE LIVE ROOT AND NOT THE VIEW. The kit's four skill rows source
         `.claude/skills/<id>` in FS-GG/.github `registry/repos.yml`, and `registry/repos.lock`
         content-addresses those exact paths — so the materialize writes there, and
         `coordination-coherence` grades those bytes against this repo's pin. Making `.agents/skills`
         the live root instead would rename a `kit:` source, obliging a kit republish and a
         seven-receiver fan-out for a path rename. See FS-GG/.github
         `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` §3.

         BOTH PROPERTIES ARE DECLARED EXPLICITLY, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
         `scripts/skill-view check` (with the `receiver-proj` flag) grades their union on this repo's
         required `Build + test` check and fails CLOSED on an absent property — because an undeclared
         property would silently evaluate to the package default and hide exactly the regression that
         alarm exists for. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.props (CPM; Renovate-managed), like every other pin. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes. A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to
    a plain text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts
    without one: a clean clone, a new worktree, and `kit-materialize.yml`'s runner alike. Measured on a
    fresh clone of this repo at the retirement commit — without this target the materialize FAILS with
    "view skill root '.agents/skills' is ABSENT or a DANGLING link", and this repo's next Renovate kit
    bump would red on a tree nobody touched.

    So the generate belongs next to the assertion, not in a workflow step that only some of those trees
    run. The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the
    FS.GG.Kit version this repo pins (a `kind: client` kit row since 0.15.0), never one fetched from a
    moving hub `main`: a receiver's verdict must be a function of the receiver (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override, so it
    would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots this
    target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts. That same emptying is
    what `scripts/skill-view check` (with the `receiver-proj` flag) reds on.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggAudioGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
